\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Degree-4k cost at $W=1280$. Seconds are frozen selection measurements averaged over five paths. Verification savings use the separately measured setup plus frozen world, selection and evaluation times; they are hybrid estimates, not direct end-to-end trials.}}{11}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cost}{{5}{11}{Degree-4k cost at $W=1280$. Seconds are frozen selection measurements averaged over five paths. Verification savings use the separately measured setup plus frozen world, selection and evaluation times; they are hybrid estimates, not direct end-to-end trials}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Computational mechanism of candidate-restriction evaluation. A ranking cutoff produces the eligible pool $C(\tau )$ while propagation remains on the complete processed graph. The two colored paths identify distinct resolution choices: finite selection budget $W$ can change the full reference $S_{\mathrm  {full}}(W)$, whereas the PageRank tolerance $\tau $ can exchange near-cutoff candidates before restricted-pool greedy selection. The resulting sets are selected with the same graph and paired selection worlds, then compared by spread on a disjoint evaluation family. The ca-GrQc anchor reports the observed exchange of two members of the 20-node PageRank pool and a changed selected set on all five paths at $W=1280$; its numerical differences are reported in Fig.~\ref {fig:precision-budget}. The diagram is a computational dependency map, not a causal model of real diffusion. Agreement, independent utility and scoped cost are deliberately kept distinct.}}{13}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanism}{{1}{13}{Computational mechanism of candidate-restriction evaluation. A ranking cutoff produces the eligible pool $C(\tau )$ while propagation remains on the complete processed graph. The two colored paths identify distinct resolution choices: finite selection budget $W$ can change the full reference $S_{\mathrm {full}}(W)$, whereas the PageRank tolerance $\tau $ can exchange near-cutoff candidates before restricted-pool greedy selection. The resulting sets are selected with the same graph and paired selection worlds, then compared by spread on a disjoint evaluation family. The ca-GrQc anchor reports the observed exchange of two members of the 20-node PageRank pool and a changed selected set on all five paths at $W=1280$; its numerical differences are reported in Fig.~\ref {fig:precision-budget}. The diagram is a computational dependency map, not a causal model of real diffusion. Agreement, independent utility and scoped cost are deliberately kept distinct}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Frozen main-matrix incremental loss relative to each budget's full reference; PageRank uses default tolerance. Points are means of five computational paths; bars are path standard deviations. Random-pool repeats are averaged within each path first. Negative loss denotes independently measured gain against the finite reference. Graph panels retain a common percentage scale. Complete method summaries and source data accompany Online Resource 1}}{14}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:loss}{{2}{14}{Frozen main-matrix incremental loss relative to each budget's full reference; PageRank uses default tolerance. Points are means of five computational paths; bars are path standard deviations. Random-pool repeats are averaged within each path first. Negative loss denotes independently measured gain against the finite reference. Graph panels retain a common percentage scale. Complete method summaries and source data accompany Online Resource 1}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Post-hoc PageRank budget--precision sensitivity. Each point is the mean incremental loss over the original five paths; bars are path standard deviations. Default and strict refer to tolerances $10^{-6}$ and $10^{-12}$; strict pools agree at $10^{-14}$. Negative loss favors the restricted set. The panels show why the ca-GrQc default direction reversal cannot be generalized to the strict ranking, while CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote retain their named patterns. This is a diagnostic comparison, not a new primary test family.}}{15}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:precision-budget}{{3}{15}{Post-hoc PageRank budget--precision sensitivity. Each point is the mean incremental loss over the original five paths; bars are path standard deviations. Default and strict refer to tolerances $10^{-6}$ and $10^{-12}$; strict pools agree at $10^{-14}$. Negative loss favors the restricted set. The panels show why the ca-GrQc default direction reversal cannot be generalized to the strict ranking, while CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote retain their named patterns. This is a diagnostic comparison, not a new primary test family}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Full-reference paired spread changes for adjacent budgets. Each graph contributes paths 1101--1105 from left to right. Filled circles mark changed final sets; hollow squares mark unchanged sets. Bars are unadjusted 95\% normal conditional Monte Carlo intervals from 10,000 paired worlds. Panels use different vertical ranges to display the measured changes; these are not across-network confidence intervals}}{16}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:transitions}{{4}{16}{Full-reference paired spread changes for adjacent budgets. Each graph contributes paths 1101--1105 from left to right. Filled circles mark changed final sets; hollow squares mark unchanged sets. Bars are unadjusted 95\% normal conditional Monte Carlo intervals from 10,000 paired worlds. Panels use different vertical ranges to display the measured changes; these are not across-network confidence intervals}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Descriptive loss--cost decisions for the frozen default-tolerance matrix at $W=1280$. Cells count paths whose point-estimate incremental loss is at or below the column tolerance. Row labels report mean hybrid verification-inclusive saving across all five paths, whether or not they pass. Random rows first average three pools within each path and use the mean cost of one random-pool deployment. The tolerances are illustrative, not justified application thresholds or noninferiority tests}}{17}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cost}{{5}{17}{Descriptive loss--cost decisions for the frozen default-tolerance matrix at $W=1280$. Cells count paths whose point-estimate incremental loss is at or below the column tolerance. Row labels report mean hybrid verification-inclusive saving across all five paths, whether or not they pass. Random rows first average three pools within each path and use the mean cost of one random-pool deployment. The tolerances are illustrative, not justified application thresholds or noninferiority tests}{figure.5}{}}
